WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:08.700
RAHUL MEHENDALE: Hello, my
name is Rahul Mehendale,

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.060
and I lead New Business Creation
within Deloitte's life sciences

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:13.680
and health practice.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.320
With me is my colleague
Jurgen Planck,

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.850
who is our precision
medicine leader.

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:23.960
We are here today to examine the
future of biomedical research.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:27.270
We are going to look both at
how biomedical research has been

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:30.810
conducted to date and how
the landscape is changing,

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:35.340
as well as a potential solution
that we are using to address

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:37.400
this innovative field.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:40.520
Biomedical research is
at a unique juncture.

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:43.190
It is increasingly
turning to data science

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:45.920
to make new discoveries
for treatments.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:48.530
Let me give you three
examples of drivers

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:50.690
that are causing this
change and the resulting

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:52.460
needs for data science.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:55.440
First, there are
large volumes of data

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:59.270
now available, orders of
magnitude more than before,

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:02.860
that need to be stored
for efficient use.

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:05.880
This includes the traditional
sets of science-generated data,

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:09.730
such as genomics data,
imaging data, clinical data--

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:13.050
but also patient-generated
data from variables,

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:16.630
and environmental data
and exposure data.

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:18.570
These data are now
being collected

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:22.740
in ever-increasing quantities,
and that is an expectation

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:25.320
that if they can be easily
stored and analyzed,

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:28.230
they can help drive new
discoveries and treatment

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:29.760
options.

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:33.990
Thus, there is a huge need for
storage and efficient search

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:37.270
capabilities for
biomedical data.

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:39.910
Second there is an
interest in driving

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:42.760
deeper into precision
medicine by using

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:45.320
artificial intelligence.

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:48.470
Researchers want to understand
how the body functions

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:49.760
at all levels--

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:53.210
starting at the molecular level,
then at the cellular level

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:55.510
and the organ level.

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:58.490
Take cystic fibrosis
as an example.

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:01.970
Researchers found a correlation
between the CFTR gene

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:04.820
and the disease, leading
to the understanding

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:07.310
that a mutation in
the CFTR gene was

00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:10.360
the cause of cystic fibrosis.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:13.600
From this discovery, a new
treatment could be developed.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:17.530
And two years ago, the FDA
granted a breakthrough therapy

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:20.500
designation to a particular
gene therapy that

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:23.540
addressed the mutated gene.

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:26.090
However, in most
cases, the relation

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:30.750
between genetic mutation and
disease is much more complex.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:36.450
It is not a one mutated gene
causes one disease situation.

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:38.920
So researchers
need to go further.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:41.670
They need to understand
how the body functions

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:44.640
at the molecular level,
looking at all genes

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:49.040
and proteins in action
within cells and organs.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:50.740
This will require
the development

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:54.640
of new artificial intelligence
methods that can analyze

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:56.740
all of the data available.

00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:59.680
Thus, biomedical
research is becoming

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:02.350
a huge computational task.

00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:06.340
Third, there are an estimated
7,000 rare diseases,

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:10.600
of which only 500 have
treatments-- and even fewer

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:11.650
have cures.

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:14.170
Thus, there is an urgent
call to researchers

00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:15.950
to share all of their data.

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:19.090
The rapidly advanced the
discovery of new treatments.

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:21.300
While each of these
7,000 diseases

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:24.370
may be rare individually,
the total tally

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:28.210
affects a significant number
across the population.

00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:33.020
Do note-- be learned that
cancer is not just one disease,

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:36.190
but rather, it is a collection
of many different types

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:39.580
of cancer, some of
which are very rare.

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:41.530
We need to understand
what is happening

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:44.500
to the body of each of
these different cancer types

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:46.850
in order to find cures.

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:49.150
By sharing their data with
the research community,

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:52.190
there is an opportunity
to perform new analysis

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:54.410
and make new discoveries.

00:03:54.410 --> 00:03:57.440
Let me summarize-- to
make new discoveries

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:00.890
and find new treatments,
biomedical researchers

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:05.270
will need to be able to
store analyze, and share

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:08.450
all of their data at scale.

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:11.660
We are now going to look at
a particular use case that

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:14.990
focuses on the necessary
steps to share data

00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:17.540
without compromising privacy.

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:20.079
The biomedical data
we use for research

00:04:20.079 --> 00:04:21.709
are collected from humans.

00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:25.650
And ensuring the privacy of
these data donors is critical.

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:30.470
Without it, we would put at risk
the trust given to scientists

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:32.830
by these data donors.

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:36.110
The HIPAA privacy rule
establishes the conditions

00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:39.860
under which protected health
information or PHI maybe

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:43.190
used or disclosed for
research purposes.

00:04:43.190 --> 00:04:48.320
In short, HIPAA requires
that all PHI be identified

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:50.570
before data are shared.

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:54.110
In addition, other laws
require that all personally

00:04:54.110 --> 00:05:00.030
identifiable information
PII also be de-identified.

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:03.990
Our use case focuses on the
identification of imaging data

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:08.220
for cancer, cardiovascular,
and pulmonary research--

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:10.350
a critical step
before these images

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:13.210
can be shared that the
research community.

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:18.260
On this slide, you will
see a pair of lung x-rays.

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:21.920
Today, most CT scans,
MRIs, and x-rays

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:26.270
use the DICOM standard, which
stands for digital imaging

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:28.210
and communications in medicine.

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:32.990
DICOM data has a header, which
includes PHI and PII data.

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:36.970
See the red boxes on
the top left and right.

00:05:36.970 --> 00:05:42.120
In addition, the PHI and PII can
be found on the image itself.

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:45.020
See the red box at the bottom.

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:47.690
We are working with
our client on a pilot

00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:52.190
to ensure that patients' data
is de-identified by removing

00:05:52.190 --> 00:05:56.660
masking or encrypting the
sensitive PHI PII data

00:05:56.660 --> 00:05:59.310
before images are shared.

00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:03.130
To do this, we are using
Google's health care API

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:06.180
as an out of the box solution.

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:10.600
In our pilot, we are working
to answer two questions.

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:13.960
How reliable is the
de-identification process?

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:18.850
And what is achievable
throughput of the process?

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:23.240
This is important as we are
working to do this at scale.

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:26.990
As you can see, de-identifying
data just from images

00:06:26.990 --> 00:06:29.090
is a large undertaking.

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:31.700
And this is only
one of many steps

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:35.240
that must be taken to prepare
data for large scale analytic

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:36.410
efforts.

00:06:36.410 --> 00:06:39.320
If we take a step back and
look at all the steps required

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:42.740
for biomedical research, we
realize that what we need

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:44.780
is an efficient
solution that provides

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:48.570
the biomedical community with
the ability to store, analyze,

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:51.530
and share their data at scale.

00:06:51.530 --> 00:06:55.280
We will now discuss how we can
build such an environment that

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:58.670
meets these needs on the
Google Cloud platform.

00:06:58.670 --> 00:07:02.120
However, we should note that
a technical solution alone

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:04.970
will not be sufficient
to create a timing

00:07:04.970 --> 00:07:09.930
ecosystem of biomedical research
that widely shares their data.

00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:12.050
There are several
non-technical challenges

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:15.020
that must be overcome, but
they are beyond the scope

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:16.980
of this presentation.

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:19.340
I'm now going to
hand over to Juergen

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:22.115
to walk us through the details
of the GCP-based solution.

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:28.690
JUERGEN KLENK: Thanks, Rahul.

00:07:28.690 --> 00:07:31.030
To start, let's
examine the principles

00:07:31.030 --> 00:07:34.540
of a biomedical research
data science solution.

00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:37.180
As Rahul mentioned,
there are three

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:39.490
key functional requirements--

00:07:39.490 --> 00:07:43.450
the ability to effectively
store and manage data,

00:07:43.450 --> 00:07:46.750
the ability to
analyze data at scale,

00:07:46.750 --> 00:07:50.320
and the ability to share
data with the community

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:53.650
and effectively
use to share data.

00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:55.360
Let's look at each
of these three

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:59.630
in turn, starting with
data organization.

00:07:59.630 --> 00:08:03.770
A foundational principle for
organizing by medical data

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:07.020
is the concept of FAIR data.

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:12.390
Fair stands for findable,
accessible, interoperable,

00:08:12.390 --> 00:08:14.750
and reusable.

00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:18.540
Let's look at what each of
these mean, F for findable,

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:21.440
A for accessible, I
for interoperable,

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:26.660
and R for reusable,
starting with findable.

00:08:26.660 --> 00:08:31.580
This means that all data must
be annotated with metadata

00:08:31.580 --> 00:08:33.860
that makes the data searchable.

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:37.590
This must be possible
for all data types,

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:40.380
the data should be
stored in a data lake.

00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:44.230
And we need a proper
metadata repository.

00:08:44.230 --> 00:08:47.050
Data that is newly
ingested should ideally

00:08:47.050 --> 00:08:52.120
be annotated with metadata in an
automated or semi-automated way

00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:56.520
to allow data ingest at scale.

00:08:56.520 --> 00:08:59.870
Finally, we need an
efficient search mechanism

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:03.260
that lets us discover
data of interest.

00:09:03.260 --> 00:09:06.410
This must be possible
for all data.

00:09:06.410 --> 00:09:10.330
Today, most data sit in silos
that cannot be searched from

00:09:10.330 --> 00:09:12.920
the outside.

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:15.800
The next one is
A for accessible.

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:18.830
This means that there
must be an easy way

00:09:18.830 --> 00:09:22.160
to request access to data that
has been discovered and is

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:23.840
of interest.

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:26.840
Ideally, we don't want
to move the data around,

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:29.540
so data access
really means that I

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:32.240
can use the data
to compute on it,

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:35.420
and quite possibly,
compute across many data

00:09:35.420 --> 00:09:39.160
sets that may reside
in different locations.

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:41.790
What we have today,
again, is still mostly

00:09:41.790 --> 00:09:45.570
data silos, where it is
impossible to run algorithms

00:09:45.570 --> 00:09:49.000
across the data sets.

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:54.020
The third fair data concept
is I for interoperable.

00:09:54.020 --> 00:09:57.270
Now this is where data
standards come into play.

00:09:57.270 --> 00:10:00.160
Unfortunately, in
biomedical research,

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:03.290
there is a myriad of standards.

00:10:03.290 --> 00:10:07.310
At a minimum, data should
adhere to a common standard,

00:10:07.310 --> 00:10:10.850
and we need mappings between
the most common standards.

00:10:10.850 --> 00:10:13.460
And yes, by data
standards, I also

00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:17.480
mean semantic standards such as
ontology and vocabularies, not

00:10:17.480 --> 00:10:21.120
just syntactic standards.

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:26.020
I is probably the hardest
of the four fair principles.

00:10:26.020 --> 00:10:28.960
Finally, R for reusable.

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:32.200
This has to do with data use
agreements and consents that

00:10:32.200 --> 00:10:34.610
accompany the data.

00:10:34.610 --> 00:10:39.380
It must be possible to trace
what data are used for so

00:10:39.380 --> 00:10:41.810
that any misuse
can be discovered

00:10:41.810 --> 00:10:44.490
in appropriate measures
to protect the data

00:10:44.490 --> 00:10:46.730
and their human data
donors can be taken.

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:52.860
When it comes to the second
requirement, powerful data

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:57.720
analysis, we have to realize
that algorithms are actually

00:10:57.720 --> 00:11:00.240
just data, as well.

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:04.110
And thus, their code should
also follow fair principles,

00:11:04.110 --> 00:11:08.380
because in the end, we not only
want to share the raw data,

00:11:08.380 --> 00:11:10.830
but also, our analysis
methods in order

00:11:10.830 --> 00:11:13.740
to accelerate innovation.

00:11:13.740 --> 00:11:16.600
We need an easy-to-use
data science environment

00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:19.980
that allows us to scalable
compute across all

00:11:19.980 --> 00:11:22.140
of the data of interests.

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:25.740
Furthermore, we want to
be able to reuse already

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:27.840
existing algorithms.

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:30.060
Many of these are
encoded in the form

00:11:30.060 --> 00:11:33.480
of services that are natively
available on the Google Cloud

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:35.420
platform.

00:11:35.420 --> 00:11:38.470
The previously discussed the
de-identification service

00:11:38.470 --> 00:11:40.330
is such an algorithm.

00:11:40.330 --> 00:11:43.720
And we need to be able to
access it programmatically

00:11:43.720 --> 00:11:47.810
through its API in an easy way.

00:11:47.810 --> 00:11:50.660
Many other algorithms are
bundled into libraries.

00:11:50.660 --> 00:11:53.600
This could be machine learning
and artificial intelligence

00:11:53.600 --> 00:11:59.390
methods for image analysis
or for a genomic study.

00:11:59.390 --> 00:12:01.880
Again, these
algorithms or libraries

00:12:01.880 --> 00:12:04.730
must be easily accessible
from within the data science

00:12:04.730 --> 00:12:07.130
environment.

00:12:07.130 --> 00:12:10.520
The last requirement
is data sharing.

00:12:10.520 --> 00:12:13.880
We already discussed this as
part of the FAIR principles.

00:12:13.880 --> 00:12:17.510
But to reiterate,
sharing must be possible

00:12:17.510 --> 00:12:21.500
not only within teams, but
also, across teams and even

00:12:21.500 --> 00:12:23.980
between institutions.

00:12:23.980 --> 00:12:25.950
If we follow these
guiding principles

00:12:25.950 --> 00:12:29.400
when building a solution on
the Google Cloud platform,

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:32.880
we will have enabled the
biomedical research community

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:35.610
to focus on what
they are best at--

00:12:35.610 --> 00:12:37.500
doing their research
without having

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:40.680
to worry about the
underlying technology.

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:43.260
And hopefully, be able
to accelerate discoveries

00:12:43.260 --> 00:12:46.710
and new treatment options for
the many untreatable diseases

00:12:46.710 --> 00:12:47.380
that ail us.

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:55.020
We have developed a CGP-native
reference implementation

00:12:55.020 --> 00:12:58.590
for biomedical research solution
that follows the principles we

00:12:58.590 --> 00:13:00.590
just discussed.

00:13:00.590 --> 00:13:03.530
You can see some
screenshots on the right.

00:13:03.530 --> 00:13:07.070
And I will show an
architectural diagram later on.

00:13:07.070 --> 00:13:11.720
The data types we have to
handle include clinical data

00:13:11.720 --> 00:13:14.660
in the form of electronic
health records,

00:13:14.660 --> 00:13:20.610
often now following the
FHIR standard, genomic data,

00:13:20.610 --> 00:13:25.170
medical and scientific
imaging data, radiology images

00:13:25.170 --> 00:13:26.760
following the DICOM standard.

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:28.950
Pathology images
do not consistently

00:13:28.950 --> 00:13:31.830
follow that standard.

00:13:31.830 --> 00:13:35.590
And we have also handled medical
supply chain and public health

00:13:35.590 --> 00:13:38.830
data labs, mostly
as part of our work

00:13:38.830 --> 00:13:41.080
for the COVID-19 health
care coalition, which

00:13:41.080 --> 00:13:45.280
is a pro bono effort of almost
1,000 organizations coming

00:13:45.280 --> 00:13:47.140
together to share
data and perform

00:13:47.140 --> 00:13:48.795
studies to combat COVID-19.

00:13:51.840 --> 00:13:56.040
We're also supporting a
variety of analytic methods.

00:13:56.040 --> 00:13:59.730
Researchers often prefer to
use a notebook-like environment

00:13:59.730 --> 00:14:02.940
to coat their methods and
workflows, specifically

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:07.930
Jupyter Notebooks that can
be coded in R or in Python.

00:14:07.930 --> 00:14:10.250
To select data sets
and those cohorts,

00:14:10.250 --> 00:14:13.570
but also to generate
downstream analysis,

00:14:13.570 --> 00:14:17.150
BigQuery can be easily used.

00:14:17.150 --> 00:14:21.730
We also discussed access to
micro services via their APIs

00:14:21.730 --> 00:14:25.140
the Google Cloud platform
through its many health

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:27.910
care and biomedical
products, offers

00:14:27.910 --> 00:14:31.240
access to many services
ranging from genomic analysis

00:14:31.240 --> 00:14:34.210
to data mapping
to image analytics

00:14:34.210 --> 00:14:37.420
to advanced machine learning
and artificial intelligence

00:14:37.420 --> 00:14:39.070
and more.

00:14:39.070 --> 00:14:42.100
These are powerful
tools that we can bring

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:44.840
to the researchers' fingertips.

00:14:44.840 --> 00:14:47.330
And finally, we
offer the ability

00:14:47.330 --> 00:14:51.050
to reuse existing workflows
through sharing, enabling

00:14:51.050 --> 00:14:56.100
true collaboration in method
and algorithm development.

00:14:59.780 --> 00:15:02.130
Now let's take a closer
look at the solution.

00:15:02.130 --> 00:15:04.480
You can see here
two screenshots.

00:15:04.480 --> 00:15:07.190
The left screenshot
shows the home screen,

00:15:07.190 --> 00:15:10.280
where you can see all
of your projects listed.

00:15:10.280 --> 00:15:12.860
Projects are the big
tiles on the screen, which

00:15:12.860 --> 00:15:16.400
show the project name,
a short description,

00:15:16.400 --> 00:15:19.720
and the approved users.

00:15:19.720 --> 00:15:23.970
Of the right screenshow,
we open one project.

00:15:23.970 --> 00:15:28.080
At the top, you can see the
project information again.

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:30.270
On the left is the
metadata repository,

00:15:30.270 --> 00:15:34.380
which is searchable, and where
it can select data for use

00:15:34.380 --> 00:15:37.880
as well as share
data with others.

00:15:37.880 --> 00:15:40.560
And on the right side
are the workspaces,

00:15:40.560 --> 00:15:43.280
where it can launch Jupyter
Notebooks to perform

00:15:43.280 --> 00:15:46.340
your analyses.

00:15:46.340 --> 00:15:50.530
What's important is that this
user interface is intuitive

00:15:50.530 --> 00:15:53.560
and takes away from the
complexity of the Google Cloud

00:15:53.560 --> 00:15:56.060
Platform itself.

00:15:56.060 --> 00:16:00.580
If I showed the GCP home screen
to a biomedical researcher,

00:16:00.580 --> 00:16:03.580
they would be overwhelmed
by the choices of tools

00:16:03.580 --> 00:16:07.150
and often not understand
how to use them.

00:16:07.150 --> 00:16:10.510
Our user interface provides
them with an intuitive screen

00:16:10.510 --> 00:16:14.590
that they can understand how to
navigate and perform that work

00:16:14.590 --> 00:16:17.590
all the while using the
full power of GCP behind it.

00:16:21.800 --> 00:16:24.630
Let's take a look
at the architecture.

00:16:24.630 --> 00:16:27.560
I'll start on the
left side, this

00:16:27.560 --> 00:16:31.730
is where we receive data from
systems such as microscopes

00:16:31.730 --> 00:16:35.060
or other devices that can
stream their data right

00:16:35.060 --> 00:16:37.250
into our environment.

00:16:37.250 --> 00:16:39.190
We can use a command
line interface

00:16:39.190 --> 00:16:42.740
to push the data with
a defined frequency,

00:16:42.740 --> 00:16:45.720
or we can manually
load the data.

00:16:45.720 --> 00:16:50.030
The ingest process uses cloud
network and Cloud Data Fusion

00:16:50.030 --> 00:16:55.320
to push the data into a
Google Cloud Storage bucket.

00:16:55.320 --> 00:16:59.080
The metadata repository near
the middle of this slide

00:16:59.080 --> 00:17:03.040
serves as an environment to
store all metadata associated

00:17:03.040 --> 00:17:05.720
with the collected data.

00:17:05.720 --> 00:17:10.290
Upon data ingest, data can
be inspected for tags--

00:17:10.290 --> 00:17:13.730
for example, FIHR
or DICOM tags that

00:17:13.730 --> 00:17:16.490
can be used to automatically
generate metadata.

00:17:19.020 --> 00:17:23.400
We use services provided by
the Google health care API

00:17:23.400 --> 00:17:26.990
to the left of the
metadata repository

00:17:26.990 --> 00:17:30.230
to perform initial
transformations of the data

00:17:30.230 --> 00:17:33.580
such as de-identification.

00:17:33.580 --> 00:17:35.490
This requires
temporary placement

00:17:35.490 --> 00:17:38.550
of the data into a
FIHR or DICOM store

00:17:38.550 --> 00:17:41.100
where the data identification
service is being performed.

00:17:44.160 --> 00:17:46.610
We have a data sharing
capability that invokes

00:17:46.610 --> 00:17:50.700
[INAUDIBLE],, Firebase,
and can leverage services,

00:17:50.700 --> 00:17:52.340
such as Google's
data marketplace

00:17:52.340 --> 00:17:55.230
to make data discoverable.

00:17:55.230 --> 00:18:01.660
For search, near the top of
the slide, we use Apache solar.

00:18:01.660 --> 00:18:05.850
And to the right, we have
notebook services that utilize

00:18:05.850 --> 00:18:09.990
GCP's1 JupyterLab to
launch notebook services.

00:18:09.990 --> 00:18:13.500
In these notebooks all
the way on the right,

00:18:13.500 --> 00:18:16.380
in the second box from
the top, the researchers

00:18:16.380 --> 00:18:19.380
can code and execute
their algorithms.

00:18:19.380 --> 00:18:21.720
They can also
access GCP services

00:18:21.720 --> 00:18:25.680
through what we call convenience
methods, which are wrappers

00:18:25.680 --> 00:18:29.850
around the API is so that
researchers don't have to learn

00:18:29.850 --> 00:18:34.170
the detailed command syntax
of an API call, but instead,

00:18:34.170 --> 00:18:36.990
can use a very simple
and intuitive command

00:18:36.990 --> 00:18:43.080
such as de-identify with
some simple arguments.

00:18:43.080 --> 00:18:47.160
For security, we use the
native GCP security layer,

00:18:47.160 --> 00:18:49.860
which provides identity
and access management

00:18:49.860 --> 00:18:54.280
and offers a very granular
roll-based data access.

00:18:54.280 --> 00:18:56.470
Cost management is
also built in to1

00:18:56.470 --> 00:18:58.930
allow researchers to see
the cost of the service

00:18:58.930 --> 00:19:01.500
before they use it.

00:19:01.500 --> 00:19:05.730
Above all, we strive to follow
the open community's best

00:19:05.730 --> 00:19:07.470
practices.

00:19:07.470 --> 00:19:10.950
Data will not end up
in a proprietary silo.

00:19:10.950 --> 00:19:16.750
Rather, it sits in a GCS bucket
and can be accessed and reused

00:19:16.750 --> 00:19:19.180
from any other environment.

00:19:19.180 --> 00:19:23.520
Similarly, Compute uses
native GCP components,

00:19:23.520 --> 00:19:25.860
which will work across
data sets stored in GCS.

00:19:28.430 --> 00:19:31.100
What we hope to illustrate with
this reference implementation

00:19:31.100 --> 00:19:33.290
is to demonstrate
how we can stop

00:19:33.290 --> 00:19:37.310
building more proprietary data
silos in biomedical research

00:19:37.310 --> 00:19:39.830
that will just prevent the
community from collaborating

00:19:39.830 --> 00:19:41.840
efficiently.

00:19:41.840 --> 00:19:45.350
If we follow the standards and
best practices we laid out,

00:19:45.350 --> 00:19:48.410
solutions like our
reference implementation

00:19:48.410 --> 00:19:51.860
will enable the construction
of a collaborative biomedical

00:19:51.860 --> 00:19:55.550
research environment that can
help researchers accelerate

00:19:55.550 --> 00:19:58.500
discoveries and treatment.

00:19:58.500 --> 00:20:03.385
Now I'll hand it back to Rahul
to close out our discussion.

00:20:03.385 --> 00:20:05.260
RAHUL MEHENDALE: Thanks,
Juergen. And thanks,

00:20:05.260 --> 00:20:08.560
Google, for the opportunity
for this discussion today.

00:20:08.560 --> 00:20:12.040
In summary, we have reviewed
how biomedical research

00:20:12.040 --> 00:20:15.160
is transforming
into a data science.

00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:18.190
We discussed the principles
of how biomedical data can

00:20:18.190 --> 00:20:20.320
be effectively
managed and analyzed

00:20:20.320 --> 00:20:21.970
in the Google Cloud platform.

00:20:21.970 --> 00:20:25.480
And we showed you a
reference implementation.

00:20:25.480 --> 00:20:29.320
In addition, we discussed
how Google Cloud platform

00:20:29.320 --> 00:20:32.050
allows us to do
biomedical research

00:20:32.050 --> 00:20:35.470
in a highly scalable
and cost-effective way

00:20:35.470 --> 00:20:37.320
in the cloud.

00:20:37.320 --> 00:20:39.750
And finally and
most importantly,

00:20:39.750 --> 00:20:42.560
a cloud-native solution
for researchers

00:20:42.560 --> 00:20:46.040
to collaborate and share
their data with one another

00:20:46.040 --> 00:20:50.030
is widely important to
accelerate scientific discovery

00:20:50.030 --> 00:20:53.235
and find new treatments for
the many still untreatable

00:20:53.235 --> 00:20:53.735
diseases.

00:20:57.430 --> 00:20:59.640
If you have any questions
on this presentation

00:20:59.640 --> 00:21:03.990
or the content, please feel free
to reach out to us directly.

00:21:03.990 --> 00:21:07.800
We have included our contact
information on this slide.

00:21:07.800 --> 00:21:11.120
Thanks again for your
time and interest.

